<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Citarum River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Citarum River is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, flowing from the highlands south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. Sundanese tradition, chiefly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a>, holds that around 669 the lands of Tarumanagara were divided along it, the Sunda Kingdom taking the west and Galuh the east. Its lower basin holds early sites such as the Buddhist temples of Batujaya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/citarum-river.jpg" alt="Citarum River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS022-E-63295_-_View_of_Java.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
